se as to where  the problem was happening and that  itself takes a long time and it  increases the outage period  so one of the biggest requests from  customers was that if you could just  tell us whether  the root cause of the failure is it  google or is it us  or is it the network that itself would  be a huge value add  the other problem was that it's very  hard to understand that when  any of your admins are making  configuration changes  what is going to be the impact of it  whether these are rock-solid config  changes or is something going to fail  so the what of the connectivity test is  uh  it's a tool to help you diagnose  connectivity issues  uh before you act can actually do it or  even after you are you have done it  saying these are the conflicts that have  changed and as a result  uh these are the failures uh between a  source or destination or to even  validate  that yes this is the flow that you were  expecting and it is doing it as you  expected  how do we do it so we don't actually run  a ping or a trace route so this is  complete static modeling of your network  using  uh your configuration uh files and your  routing information and so on  so uh here is an example of a  connectivity test  or you can have like source and desk  protocols or  protocol and ports uh either you can  specify  an ip address or as a source and  destination or you can have  vms a source vm and a desk vm or with  their respective ports which is optional  and  and also protocol field which is  optional uh and what we give to you  is a step-by-step visualization about  what path your packet would be taking  and what configurations it would be  hitting  uh so in this case it is starting from a  vm instance there is an egress firewall  that it hits  uh which it passes then there is a  static route lookup that happens  which it passes uh and uh then there is  a nat  lookup which is which is where it fails  that maybe there is a missing net rule  so here are a few examples of  connectivity tests  that we have shown like different paths  that it can take  uh the first one is uh it goes through  a firewall rule or it is successful and  if there is no reason  for the failure and it says the packet  is dropped it could also be that  it goes outside your im policy domain  that it allows you and you may want to  reach out to your super admin who has  the policies because of which you  between both of you you could understand  the reason uh  the second example is in this case it is  going through  a firewall rule uh and then it is  getting dropped  uh in the third example it is going  through um  it is going through a load balancer uh  and then it is getting dropped  and the fourth one is a successful  example that we have taken where it goes  through  a vpn tunnel uh and then it uh  comes in right and we show you the  different steps just like our trace  route would  except that in this case um a trace  route shows ip addresses versus here  you see all the steps of the  configurations that would be executed on  that packet right be it of  vpn tunnel beat a routing look up be it  a natural look up or a firewall rule  and in case of a nat and a fireball rule  failure or even  a success we would tell you exactly  which rule it hit  and why it was a success or a failure so  you could go back in real time and say  like oh  the survival rule is accidentally  blocking this packet and that's not what  i intended you go change that and then  you will see it immediately that  your packet flow is successful so let's  talk about the next module  uh this is called firewall insights so  one of the other  challenges that we heard from many of  you uh  is around firewalls and the management  of firewall groups  uh several people had like thousands and  thousands of firewall rules  and if they wanted to optimize it they  did not know where to start  because usually what we heard was there  is a series of admins some security  admin in the past must have created thi