s because you  have an  overarching uh allow rule that is shadow  that is  you know uh addressing this allow uh  functionality or desire so  that's a very simple example of  optimization but then there are complex  other more complex examples so we  analyze all of that and we show you the  shadowing and shadowed relationship  uh between the rules among the rules and  then  we as a result we allow you to  strengthen your firewall control  by even recommending to you um or  showing you insights on where some of  your security could be tightened  by showing you that these are the only  rules which are being  hit and so there are there's two ends of  the spectrum on one in the end of the  spectrum you are able to  optimize your firewall rules and on the  other end of the spectrum  you are able to strengthen your security  by making  uh the rules more specific um  and you could do both based on what your  desire is  now we'll go to the demo for fibo  insights  firewall insights provides you three  main things today  firstly it shows you the different  relationships among your firewall rules  the shadowing shadowed conflicting  relationships  secondly it shows you which of your deny  rules  have been hit and thirdly which of your  allowed rules have not been hit in the  last  60 days as we discussed  firewall rules can be added by different  people over time  causing new rules to be accidentally  overshadowed by existing rules  or vice versa without any automated  detection this is really hard to do  so for example if there is a vm1 in a 10  1  24 subnet uh which cannot reach vm 2  in a 10 slash 24 subnet when you  intended it to  uh the first thing you would do is run a  ping test so you go to vm1  and you can ping 10.202  and see what happens  the ping failed so let's see what could  be the root cause of this  so to troubleshoot you would go to vm 2  and view the network details  to see what are the configurations that  are applied on vm2  and you see oh there is a 10 one slash  16  deny 5 rule which is shadowing a 10 1  24 allow firewall rule naturally causing  your traffic to be  blocked and thus break there could be  multiple such instances  that are in your firewall rules and if  you have thousands of them  it's really hard to automatically detect  and find these conflicts  so firewall insights in network  intelligence center  provides you exactly that it gives you a  list of all your shadowed rules  in your firewall configs in your vpc and  which you can audit and figure out which  ones to clean and keep  or which ones to keep so a single rule  or a multiple rules can be blocked by  multiple  or a combination of other rules in this  case an allow rule to tcp  ports at443 are being blocked  by or shadowed by a deny firewall rule  naturally causing this breakage to  happen  so you may want to proactively uh audit  all the shadowed rules in your firewall  conflicts and  figure out which ones match your traffic  needs and intent and which ones don't  and you don't want to keep and clean  those up  the second powerful thing that fibo  insights provides  is a list of allow rules that have not  been hit in the last six weeks  so you could come here to view a full  list of these rules  so as you can see there are multiple  rules that have not been hit in the last  six weeks  and if you ordered these these could be  rules left over by past applications  where  someone forgot to clean them up so in  this case there is a web app one which  has  a five volt allow rule uh for a zero  slash zero  ip address and uh it's not been hit in  the last six weeks so naturally  web app one probably went away and this  got left over  as a vestige hole in your network so  reviewing these rules will help you  tighten your security boundaries the  third powerful function that's provided  to you is the deny rules with heads  now these could point to unintended  abnormal traffic behaviors it could even  be attacks  into your network and to figure out  where  these attacks are coming from you could  go to the firewall logs corresponding to  the firewall rules  and see where the source of the traffic  is  and especially when you have a large  abnormal behavior you may want to  look into that traffic source either  there is a mis-configuration or  there is an attack coming from those  sources  in summary 5v insights gives you four  powerful functions  firstly it gives you real-time  visibility into your firewall rules  utilization and stats  it secondly it automatically detects  overlapping and misconfigured firewall  rules  thirdly it helps you optimize your  firewall configs  by enabling you to delete any shadowing  shadowed  conflicting or unused firewall rules  that do not match your intent  and finally it helps you tighten your  security boundaries  by detecting any unused vestige rules  that may be creating holes in your  network through a combination of all  these  firewall insights thus helps you to  optimize  and maintain the right set of firewall  rules to protect your cloud deployments  so to wrap up uh we have launched the  network intelligence center which has  gone ga  uh it includes all the four modules that  uh mansa and i talked about today  on network topology and performance  dashboard uh connectivity test and  firewall insights so as you can see um  we are get taking an approach of  proactive  uh intelligence like proactively  monitoring and operating  your network or shortening the outages  that happen  in a network uh and as a result then you  come to  trust the gcp network intelligence a  little  more right we'll trust and basically  help you  manage your network better  so as next steps here are some related  sessions uh  there is a cloud and complex session um  that you should definitely attend it's  related  uh what's new in network security uh it  will talk to you about the overall  security  initiative from gcp and uh it will talk  about  firewalls or at an uber level and also  delve into  firewall insights and if you want to you  could reach out to us  at network intelligence center at  google.com  and follow our blog on product slash  networking  thank you

