se as to where  the problem was happening and that  itself takes a long time and it  increases the outage period  so one of the biggest requests from  customers was that if you could just  tell us whether  the root cause of the failure is it  google or is it us  or is it the network that itself would  be a huge value add  the other problem was that it's very  hard to understand that when  any of your admins are making  configuration changes  what is going to be the impact of it  whether these are rock-solid config  changes or is something going to fail  so the what of the connectivity test is  uh  it's a tool to help you diagnose  connectivity issues  uh before you act can actually do it or  even after you are you have done it  saying these are the conflicts that have  changed and as a result  uh these are the failures uh between a  source or destination or to even  validate  that yes this is the flow that you were  expecting and it is doing it as you  expected  how do we do it so we don't actually run  a ping or a trace route so this is  complete static modeling of your network  using  uh your configuration uh files and your  routing information and so on  so uh here is an example of a  connectivity test  or you can have like source and desk  protocols or  protocol and ports uh either you can  specify  an ip address or as a source and  destination or you can have  vms a source vm and a desk vm or with  their respective ports which is optional  and  and also protocol field which is  optional uh and what we give to you  is a step-by-step visualization about  what path your packet would be taking  and what configurations it would be  hitting  uh so in this case it is starting from a  vm instance there is an egress firewall  that it hits  uh which it passes then there is a  static route lookup that happens  which it passes uh and uh then there is  a nat  lookup which is which is where it fails  that maybe there is a missing net rule  so here are a few examples of  connectivity tests  that we have shown like different paths  that it can take  uh the first one is uh it goes through  a firewall rule or it is successful and  if there is no reason  for the failure and it says the packet  is dropped it could also be that  it goes outside your im policy domain  that it allows you and you may want to  reach out to your super admin who has  the policies because of which you  between both of you you could understand  the reason uh  the second example is in this case it is  going through  a firewall rule uh and then it is  getting dropped  uh in the third example it is going  through um  it is going through a load balancer uh  and then it is getting dropped  and the fourth one is a successful  example that we have taken where it goes  through  a vpn tunnel uh and then it uh  comes in right and we show you the  different steps just like our trace  route would  except that in this case um a trace  route shows ip addresses versus here  you see all the steps of the  configurations that would be executed on  that packet right be it of  vpn tunnel beat a routing look up be it  a natural look up or a firewall rule  and in case of a nat and a fireball rule  failure or even  a success we would tell you exactly  which rule it hit  and why it was a success or a failure so  you could go back in real time and say  like oh  the survival rule is accidentally  blocking this packet and that's not what  i intended you go change that and then  you will see it immediately that  your packet flow is successful so let's  talk about the next module  uh this is called firewall insights so  one of the other  challenges that we heard from many of  you uh  is around firewalls and the management  of firewall groups  uh several people had like thousands and  thousands of firewall rules  and if they wanted to optimize it they  did not know where to start  because usually what we heard was there  is a series of admins some security  admin in the past must have created this  firewall rule and they went ahead to  a different role and new security admins  came on  and we said then how do you figure out  which rules are still active they said  oh  we do not delete firewall rules we just  add them  so that is that makes the firewall rules  very unmanageable and you don't even  know where to start  so as a result you end up with a massive  volume of eyeball rules  and sometimes there is over granting of  rules because you just  grant it at a higher level ip address  range even though you may only be  partially using some of it  uh there is also human error of course  and there is no way to figure out  unless there is an actual traffic uh  drop that  oh something bad had happened um  and then the other thing is like we  talked about outdated rules because  people are too afraid to change them  mainly going back to they don't know  what else could break  and what was the intention of this rule  for byte was created  so fibo insights took on all these  problems and we said okay let's tackle  that by applying intelligence to  the data that we have and the  configurations that we have  so one of the first things that we have  done is give you visibility into usage  metrics  uh of based on each firewall rule like  what has been the hit rate in the past x  number of days and so on  uh and you can see the hit count the  timestamp stamp  and you can export these this data is  exported to stackdriver where you can  set your own alerts the other part is  through  analysis and applying some symbolic  analysis on the config data  uh we are able to show you what is the  hierarchical relationship among  different rules  which rule is shadowing another rule  right like for example  there is a higher priority slash 16 rule  and then there are three lower priority  slash 24 rules which are obviously being  shadowed those  slash 16 is shadowing though or the  lower priority rules  and as a result you could easily get rid  of the  underlying three allow rules because you  have an  overarching uh allow rule that is shadow  that is  you know uh addressing this allow uh  functionality or desire so  that's a very simple example of  optimization but then there are complex  other more complex examples so we  analyze all of that and we show you the  shadowing and shadowed relationship  uh between the rules among the rules and  then  we as a result we allow you to  strengthen your firewall control  by even recommending to you um or  showing you insights on where some of  your security could be tightened  by showing you that these are the only  rules which are being  hit and so there are there's two ends of  the spectrum on one in the end of the  spectrum you are able to  optimize your firewall rules and on the  other end of the spectrum  you are able to strengthen your security  by making  uh the rules more specific um  and you could do both based on what your  desire is  now we'll go to the demo for fibo  insights  firewall insights provides you three  main things today  firstly it shows you the different  relationships among your firewall rules  the shadowing shadowed conflicting  relationships  secondly it shows you which of your deny  rules  have been hit and thirdly which of your  allowed rules have not been hit in the  last  60 days as we discussed  firewall rules can be added by different  people over time  causing new rules to be accidentally  overshadowed by existing rules  or vice versa without any automated  detection this is really hard to do  so for example if there is a vm1 in a 10  1  24 subnet uh which cannot reach vm 2  in a 10 slash 24 subnet when you  intended it to  uh the first thing you would do is run a  ping test so you go to vm1  and you can ping 10.202  and see what happens  the ping failed so let's see what could  be the root cause of this  so to troubleshoot you would go to vm 2  and view the network details  to see what are the configurations that  are applied on vm2  and you see oh there is a 10 one slash  16  deny 5 rule which is shadowing a 10 1  24 allow firewall rule naturally causing  your traffic to be  blocked and thus break there could be  multiple such instances  that are in your firewall rules and if  you have thousands of them  it's really hard to automatically detect  and find these conflicts  so firewall insights in network  intelligence center  provides you exactly that it gives you a  list of all your shadowed rules  in your firewall configs in your vpc and  which you can audit and figure out which  ones to clean and keep  or which ones to keep so a single rule  or a multiple rules can be blocked by  multiple  or a combination of other rules in this  case an allow rule to tcp  ports at443 are being blocked  by or shadowed by a deny firewall rule  naturally causing this breakage to  happen  so you may want to proactively uh audit  all the shadowed rules in your firewall  conflicts and  figure out which ones match your traffic  needs and intent and which ones don't  and you don't want to keep and clean  those up  the second powerful thing that fibo  insights provides  is a list of allow rules that have not  been hit in the last six weeks  so you could come here to view a full  list of these rules  so as you can see there are multiple  rules that have not been hit in the last  six weeks  and if you ordered these these could be  rules left over by past applications  where  someone forgot to clean them up so in  this case there is a web app one which  has  a five volt allow rule uh for a zero  slash zero  ip address and uh it's not been hit in  the last six weeks so naturally  web app one probably went away and this  got left over  as a vestige hole in your network so  reviewing these rules will help you  tighten your security boundaries the  third powerful function that's provided  to you is the deny rules with heads  now these could point to unintended  abnormal traffic behaviors it could even  be attacks  into your network and to figure out  where  these attacks are coming from you could  go to the firewall logs corresponding to  the firewall rules  and see where the source of the traffic  is  and especially when you have a large  abnormal behavior you may want to  look into that traffic source either  there is a mis-configuration or  there is an attack coming from those  sources  in summary 5v insights gives you four  powerful functions  firstly it gives you real-time  visibility into your firewall rules  utilization and stats  it secondly it automatically detects  overlapping and misconfigured firewall  rules  thirdly it helps you optimize your  firewall configs  by enabling you to delete any shadowing  shadowed  conflicting or unused firewall rules  that do not match your intent  and finally it helps you tighten your  security boundaries  by detecting any unused vestige rules  that may be creating holes in your  network through a combination of all  these  firewall insights thus helps you to  optimize  and maintain the right set of firewall  rules to protect your cloud deployments  so to wrap up uh we have launched the  network intelligence center which has  gone ga  uh it includes all the four modules that  uh mansa and i talked about today  on network topology and performance  dashboard uh connectivity test and  firewall insights so as you can see um  we are get taking an approach of  proactive  uh intelligence like proactively  monitoring and operating  your network or shortening the outages  that happen  in a network uh and as a result then you  come to  trust the gcp network intelligence a  little  more right we'll trust and basically  help you  manage your network better  so as next steps here are some related  sessions uh  there is a cloud and complex session um  that you should definitely attend it's  related  uh what's new in network security uh it  will talk to you about the overall  security  initiative from gcp and uh it will talk  about  firewalls or at an uber level and also  delve into  firewall insights and if you want to you  could reach out to us  at network intelligence center at  google.com  and follow our blog on product slash  networking  thank you
 

